{"name":"Puddle Mirror","hex":"#ccd7e1","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"puddle","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small, often temporary, pool of water, usually on a path or road. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Stagnant or polluted water."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To form a puddle.","To play or splash in a puddle."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/puddle"},{"term":"mirror","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A smooth surface, usually made of glass with reflective material painted on the underside, that reflects light so as to give an image of what is in front of it.","An object, person, or event that reflects or gives a picture of another."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To reflect, as in a mirror.","To act as a reflection of, either by being identical to, or by being identical but reversed."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mirror"}],"entries":[{"title":"Puddle","blocks":[{"text":"A puddle is a small accumulation of liquid, usually water, on a surface. It can form either by pooling in a depression on the surface, or by surface tension upon a flat surface. Puddles are often characterized by murky water or mud due to the disturbance and dissolving of surrounding sediment, primarily due to precipitation."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Begliktash_puddle_%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%88_%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B0_%E2%80%94_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%8F.jpg/330px-Begliktash_puddle_%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%88_%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B0_%E2%80%94_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%8F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puddle","wikidata":"Q152841"},{"title":"Mirror","blocks":[{"text":"A mirror, also known as a looking glass, is an object that reflects an image. Light that bounces off a mirror forms an image of whatever is in front of it, which is then focused through the lens of the eye or a camera. Mirrors reverse the direction of light at an angle equal to its incidence. This allows the viewer to see themselves or objects behind them, or even objects that are at an angle from them but out of their field of view, such as around a corner. Natural mirrors have existed since prehistoric times, such as the surface of water, but people have been manufacturing mirrors out of a variety of materials for thousands of years, like stone, metals, and glass. In modern mirrors, metals like silver or aluminium are often used due to their high reflectivity, applied as a thin coating on glass because of its naturally smooth and hard surface."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/52/Mirror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mirror","wikidata":"Q35197"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Begliktash_puddle_%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%88_%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B0_%E2%80%94_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%8F.jpg/500px-Begliktash_puddle_%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%88_%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B0_%E2%80%94_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%8F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Begliktash_puddle_%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%88_%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B0_%E2%80%94_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%8F.jpg/1280px-Begliktash_puddle_%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%88_%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0%BA%D0%B2%D0%B0_%E2%80%94_%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%8F.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Begliktash_puddle_%D0%91%D0%B5%D0%B3%D0%BB%D0%B8%D0%BA%D1%82%D0%B0%D1%88_%D0%BB%D0%BE%D0